# rules/
This directory contains the public-facing building blocks of a rule-based transaction categorisation system. `engine.py` defines immutable rules, ordered first-match-wins categorisation, and text-based rule loading; `matchers.py` supplies keyword, regular-expression, and expense-threshold predicates; and `__init__.py` presents the principal types, factories, and operations as the supported public API.

## __init__.py, engine.py, matchers.py
Start with `__init__.py` for the intended entry points: it re-exports `Rule`, `categorise`, `load_rules`, `Matcher`, `keyword`, and `regex`, while keeping implementation details out of wildcard imports. The underlying responsibilities are split between `engine.py`, which consumes matchers to define and apply rules, and `matchers.py`, which creates the callable conditions tested against transactions.

## engine.py, matchers.py
The main flow begins with matcher factories in `matchers.py`: `keyword` searches payees and notes case-insensitively, `regex` searches payees with a compiled case-insensitive expression, and `amount_below` identifies expenses below a strict cent threshold. `engine.py` packages these predicates into immutable `Rule` records, then `categorise` checks rules in order, assigns only the first matching category, and returns transaction copies without mutating the originals.

## engine.py
Use `load_rules` when rules arrive as human-readable lines rather than constructed objects. It removes comments and blank lines, validates each numbered specification, turns slash-delimited specifications into regular-expression matchers and other specifications into comma-separated keyword matchers, preserves rule order and cleaned source text, and raises `LedgerError` for malformed lines. The resulting rules are ready for `categorise`, whose first-match-wins ordering makes rule placement significant.
